<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Teixeira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1587–<a href="/years/1641">1641</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Teixeira (c. 1587–1641) was a Portuguese explorer and soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In 1637–1639 he led an expedition of some 2,000 people in canoes up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and back, the first European journey up the whole river. On the return he claimed land far to the west for Portugal, a claim later used to justify Portuguese control of most of the Amazon basin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-teixeira.jpg" alt="Pedro Teixeira" width="300" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lacobrigo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_Pedro_Teixeira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teixeira was born at the Vila of Cantanhede into a noble family and was a Knight of the Order of Christ. He married Ana Cunha in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>, daughter of Sargento-Mor Diogo de Campos Moreno, beside whom he fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a>. Arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1607, he took part in the Portuguese campaign against French Maranhão and distinguished himself at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-guaxenduba">Battle of Guaxenduba</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined Francisco Caldeira Castelo Branco's expedition that founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a>, and on 9 August 1616 set out with two armed canoes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English posts on the north shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, capturing a Dutch ship in the Xingu estuary. As interim governor of Pará in 1620-1621 he resisted Bento Maciel Parente's attempt to seize the captaincy by force. In 1623 he destroyed the Dutch fort of Mariocai and with Parente raised the fort of Santo Antônio, around which grew the town of Gurupá.</p><p>On 21 October 1625 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> at Taurege, expelling them from their last holdings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> basin. In 1626 he was ordered to procure native slaves and visited a Tapuyusús and a Tapajós village; the Tapajós refused to trade men for goods. In October 1629 the English garrison of Taurege surrendered to him, and he beat off Captain Roger North's reprisal on the fort of Santo Antônio.</p><p>The great expedition was prompted by two Franciscan friars, André de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and Domingos de La Brieba, who in 1637 travelled the river by canoe to Gurupá. The governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a>, Jácome Raimundo de Noronha, then commissioned Teixeira to explore it as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. He left Cametá on 28 October 1637 with 47 large canoes, guided by La Brieba, reached the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-negro">Rio Negro</a> in January 1638 and of the Napo on 3 July, and arrived in Quito in September 1638.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> on 16 February 1639, he founded the settlement of Franciscana on 16 August on the River Ouro, perhaps the Aguarico, to mark the limit of Portuguese control, and was back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a> on 12 December. Promoted to Capitão-Mor, he governed Pará from 28 February 1640 until 26 May 1641 and died on 4 July 1641; his ashes rest in Belém Metropolitan Cathedral. The Portuguese did not hold the Franciscana border, later fixed on the Javary River, but cited the expedition in the negotiations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1670">Treaty of Madrid</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Teixeira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pedro Teixeira's</b> expedition up the Amazon in 1637 to 1639 extended Portuguese claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-maranhao">State of Maranhão</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cantanhede, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belem">Belém</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.35,-8.59&amp;year=1622" aria-label="Cantanhede on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pedro Teixeira"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4442 4099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4442 4099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.35,-8.59&amp;year=1622">What was here in 1622? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
